message.reasoning_content:
Key bugs:

1. parse_u64 sets out=0 first, violating "out unchanged on failure" — e.g. "12x" or empty string leaves out=0. Also empty string: contract says nonempty required; parse returns true for empty (loop doesn't run) — accepts empty, violating contract. Also overflow: "18446744073789578048" wraps silently, no rejection above UINT64_MAX. Also out=0 on failure violates unchanged.

2. Registry::get returns string_view into vector while holding lock then releasing; concurrent add may push_back causing reallocation/reuse — dangling view. Also readers may retain after get returns while writers add: view invalidated by push_back move/realloc. Also names[i] unchecked bounds — but contract doesn't mention; still a defect? get with i >= size is UB; minor.

3. get returns view after releasing lock — but even single-threaded, subsequent add invalidates. Memory/thread-safety defect: dangling string_view.

4. Also vector<string> push_back with move: fine.

Minimal triggers:
- parse_u64("12x", out) with out pre-set 999 → returns false but out==0 (changed). Fix: only assign at end / accumulate in local.
- parse_u64("") returns true — accepts empty, contract violation.
- parse_u64("18446744073789578048") returns true with wrapped value — overflow not rejected.
- Registry: sv = get(0); add("x") causing realloc → sv dangling; reading sv after is UB.

Fixes: parse with local accumulator, check overflow via out > (UINT64_MAX - d)/10, reject empty, assign out only on success. Registry: return std::string by value (copy) instead of view, or keep shared; simplest: get returns std::string. Also bounds check.

Corrected parse_u64 in cpp block:

```cpp
bool parse_u64(std::string_view s, std::uint64_t& out) noexcept {
    std::uint64_t v = 0;
    if (s.empty()) return false;
    for (char c : s) {
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') return false;
        std::uint64_t d = c - '0';
        if (v > (UINT64_MAX - d) / 10) return false;
        v = v * 10 + d;
    }
    out = v;
    return true;
}
```

Note: leading zeros allowed — fine. Overflow check: v > (max-d)/10 works; edge "18446744073789578048" rejected. Also "18446744073789578047" ok.

